     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. Sertraline 2000 mg daily is 10 times the maximum recommended dose of 200 mg. I can't find a clinical reason for it in a 70 kg adult with normal kidney and liver function. If the order were given, it could cause serious harm, including serotonin syndrome, seizures, arrhythmia, and coma.\n\nHere is what I'd suggest:\n- **Check the source.** Look at the original plan or note to see whether 2000 mg was a transcription error. Common intended doses are 20, 50, 100, or 200 mg.\n- **Talk to your resident or attending.** If a prescriber really does want a dose above the maximum, they should enter it themselves. They should also document the rationale, and pharmacy will probably need to verify it.\n- **If this is an ingestion or overdose,** that is a different situation. It needs urgent evaluation and a call to poison control (1-800-222-1222 in the US). It doesn't need a scheduled daily order.\n\nOnce you have a verified dose, I'll enter it right away. For example, I can enter 50 mg by mouth once daily if that's the plan."
      }
     ],
